[cot:thought] BLS blocks bots. Web search results already provide sufficient data. Key BLS figures are
[cot:thought] now clear. Annual idle days rose sharply from 1,552 thousand in 2021 to 16,673 thousand in 2023, then dropped to 3,364 thousand in 2024 and 1,991 thousand in 2025. Workers affected grew from 458.9 thousand in 2023 to 306.8 thousand in 2025. Stoppages fell from 33 in 2023 to 30 in 2025. The 2025 service-providing sector accounted for 300,600 workers, or 98% of total stoppages.
[cot:thought] Manufacturing represented only 6,200 workers, or 2%. 2025 government employment
[cot:thought] totaled 232,800 workers and 711,600 idle days. Private employment reached 74,000 workers and 784,400 idle days.
[cot:thought] 2024 government employment was 113,300 workers and 366,700 idle days. Private employment was 158,200 workers and 2,997,400 idle days.
[cot:thought] Union density in 2025 was 10.0% overall. Public sector unionization was 32.9%. Private sector unionization was 5.9%. Local government unionization was 37.8%.
[cot:thought] Utility union coverage was 18.8%. Transportation and warehousing union coverage was 14.9%.
[cot:thought] Union density varies
[cot:thought] sharply by industry. Education and health services lead at 13.4%, followed by construction at 11.1% and manufacturing at 7.7%. Information, wholesale and retail, and finance trail at 6.6%, 4.3%, and 0.8% respectively. The
[cot:thought] next step is to pull industry-level idle days from monthly BLS listings or 2023 news reports. Panels will be built around Education and Health, Public Administration, and Manufacturing.
